-- Q1: medium / L3 — Effect of converting rental to personal use on suspended losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Kepler Investments LLC holds a rental property that generated $65,000 in suspended passive losses over three years. On June 1, 2026, the managing member converts the property to personal use rather than selling it. How does this conversion affect the suspended passive losses?', '["The $65,000 of suspended losses is permanently disallowed because the activity ceased without a taxable disposition", "The $65,000 of suspended losses is deductible in the year of conversion because the rental activity ended", "The $65,000 of suspended losses carries forward and can offset future passive income from other activities", "The $65,000 of suspended losses is added to the basis of the converted property"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(b), suspended passive losses carry forward indefinitely and can offset passive income from any activity in future years. Converting a rental to personal use is not a fully taxable disposition under Section 469(g), so the losses are not released against nonpassive income. Choice A is incorrect because suspended losses are not permanently disallowed—they remain available to offset future passive income from other sources.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Calculating passive loss offset with mixed income sources
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Nakamura, a single taxpayer, reports the following for 2026: $310,000 salary, $28,000 passive income from Birchwood Partners LP, and $53,000 passive loss from Redstone Ventures LP. Nakamura does not actively participate in any rental activities. What is Nakamura''s allowable passive loss deduction for 2026?', '["$0 deductible", "$25,000", "$28,000", "$53,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(d), passive losses are first offset against passive income. Nakamura''s $53,000 passive loss is offset by the $28,000 passive income, allowing a $28,000 deduction against the passive income. The remaining $25,000 net passive loss is suspended under Section 469(b). Choice A is incorrect because passive losses cannot offset nonpassive income such as salary; only the $28,000 of passive income is available for offset.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of recourse vs nonrecourse debt on at-risk amount
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Waverly Partners, a non-real-estate partnership, borrows $300,000 to fund operations: $180,000 is recourse debt for which partner Delgado is personally liable, and $120,000 is nonrecourse debt secured by partnership equipment. Delgado holds a 50% interest. How does the debt classification affect Delgado''s at-risk amount under Section 465?', '["Delgado''s at-risk amount includes $150,000 (50% of total debt)", "Delgado''s at-risk amount includes $90,000 from the recourse debt only", "Delgado''s at-risk amount includes $60,000 from the nonrecourse debt only", "Delgado''s at-risk amount includes $210,000 (all recourse plus half of nonrecourse)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 465(b), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount in a non-real-estate activity includes cash contributions and amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer is personally liable (recourse debt). Nonrecourse debt is excluded unless it is qualified nonrecourse financing for real estate. Delgado''s 50% share of the $180,000 recourse debt is $90,000, which is included at risk. Choice A is incorrect because the $120,000 nonrecourse debt does not increase the at-risk amount in a non-real-estate activity.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Definition of active participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(i), what does "active participation" in a rental activity require?', '["Making management decisions such as approving tenants, setting rental terms, or authorizing repairs", "Spending at least 500 hours per year on the rental activity", "Holding a general partnership interest in a real estate partnership", "Qualifying as a real estate professional under Section 469(c)(7)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(i)(6), active participation requires the taxpayer to participate in management decisions in a bona fide sense, such as approving tenants, deciding on rental terms, or approving expenditures. This is a lower standard than material participation. Choice B is incorrect because active participation has no specific hour requirement—it requires meaningful management involvement, not a quantitative threshold.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — Simple rental loss allowance calculation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Pratt, a single taxpayer with AGI of $78,000, actively participates in a rental duplex that generates a $22,000 loss in 2026. What amount of the rental loss can Pratt deduct against nonpassive income?', '["$0 deductible", "$12,000", "$22,000", "$25,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(i), taxpayers who actively participate in rental real estate may deduct up to $25,000 of rental losses against nonpassive income. The allowance begins phasing out at AGI of $100,000. Because Pratt''s AGI of $78,000 is below the phase-out threshold, the full $22,000 loss is deductible. Choice A is incorrect because the special $25,000 allowance permits deductions for active participants with AGI below $100,000.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Analyzing partial disposition and suspended loss release
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Whitfield holds a passive activity with $90,000 of suspended losses and a $140,000 adjusted basis. On October 15, 2026, Whitfield sells 40% of the interest to an unrelated buyer for $72,000 in a fully taxable transaction. What amount of suspended passive losses is released in the year of the partial sale?', '["$0 because the entire interest was not disposed of", "$36,000 proportionate to the 40% interest sold", "$90,000 because any taxable sale triggers full release", "$54,000 proportionate to the 60% retained interest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), a fully taxable disposition of an entire interest releases all suspended losses. For a partial disposition, Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-4(g) allows release of suspended losses proportionate to the interest disposed of. Whitfield sold 40% of the interest, so 40% of $90,000 = $36,000 is released. Choice C is incorrect because full release requires disposition of the taxpayer''s entire interest in the activity, not merely a partial sale.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Evaluate grouping election strategy for passive activities
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Castellano owns three passive activities: a dry-cleaning business (Activity X, $45,000 income, 400 hours participation), a car wash (Activity Y, $60,000 loss, 350 hours participation), and a laundromat (Activity Z, $30,000 loss, 280 hours participation). No single activity meets any material participation test alone. Castellano''s tax advisor recommends grouping all three under Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-4(c) as one economic unit. Evaluate the tax impact of making this grouping election.', '["Grouping converts $45,000 of net passive loss to nonpassive because aggregate participation exceeds 500 hours", "Grouping allows the $45,000 net loss to offset nonpassive income because combined hours of 1,030 meet the 500-hour material participation test", "Grouping has no effect because the combined activities still produce a net passive loss that cannot offset nonpassive income", "Grouping is prohibited because activities with both income and losses cannot be combined"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-4(c), taxpayers may group activities that form an appropriate economic unit. When grouped, Castellano''s combined hours total 1,030, exceeding the 500-hour material participation test under Temp. Reg. Section 1.469-5T(a)(1). The grouped activity produces a net $45,000 loss ($45,000 − $60,000 − $30,000), which is now nonpassive because Castellano materially participates. Choice C is incorrect because grouping changes the participation analysis—the combined 1,030 hours satisfy material participation, converting the loss to nonpassive.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Effect of at-risk recapture when liability assumed
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Donovan invested $75,000 cash in a partnership activity and deducted $60,000 of losses in prior years, reducing her at-risk amount to $15,000. In 2026, Donovan takes out a nonrecourse loan of $40,000 from the partnership that is protected by a stop-loss arrangement. What is the tax consequence under the at-risk recapture rules?', '["No effect because the loan proceeds are not treated as a distribution", "Donovan must recapture $15,000 of previously deducted losses as income under Section 465(e)", "Donovan must recapture $40,000 of previously deducted losses as income", "Donovan''s at-risk amount increases by $40,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 465(e), if a taxpayer''s at-risk amount falls below zero, previously deducted losses must be recaptured as income. The nonrecourse loan with stop-loss protection reduces Donovan''s at-risk amount by $40,000 (from $15,000 to negative $25,000), but recapture is limited to the amount that brings at-risk to zero—$15,000. Choice C is incorrect because recapture under Section 465(e) is limited to previously allowed losses that caused the at-risk amount to go below zero, not the full loan amount.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Analyzing interaction of basis and at-risk limitations
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Ortega is a 30% partner in Juniper Associates LP with a tax basis of $110,000 and an at-risk amount of $70,000. In 2026, Ortega''s distributive share of partnership loss is $95,000. After applying the basis limitation and the at-risk limitation sequentially, what is Ortega''s deductible loss before passive activity rules?', '["$70,000", "$95,000", "$110,000", "$40,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 704(d), a partner''s loss deduction is first limited to tax basis ($110,000), so $95,000 passes the basis test. Under Section 465, the loss is then limited to the at-risk amount ($70,000). The remaining $25,000 is suspended under the at-risk rules. Choice B is incorrect because although $95,000 passes the basis limitation, the at-risk limitation of $70,000 further restricts the deductible amount before passive activity rules apply.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Calculating at-risk amount with qualified nonrecourse financing
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Breckenridge Capital LLC purchases a commercial office building for $800,000. Partner Ellison contributes $120,000 cash and the partnership obtains a $680,000 qualified nonrecourse mortgage from First National Bank, secured solely by the building. Ellison holds a 25% interest. What is Ellison''s at-risk amount?', '["$120,000", "$170,000", "$200,000", "$290,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(6), qualified nonrecourse financing secured by real property used in a real estate activity is included in a taxpayer''s at-risk amount. Ellison''s at-risk amount equals her $120,000 cash contribution plus 25% of the $680,000 qualified nonrecourse mortgage ($170,000), totaling $290,000. Choice A is incorrect because it excludes the qualified nonrecourse financing, which is specifically included for real estate activities under Section 465(b)(6).', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Definition of portfolio income under Section 469
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469, which type of income is classified as portfolio income rather than passive income?', '["Rental income from a commercial warehouse", "Distributive share of partnership operating income", "Interest and dividends from publicly traded securities", "Net income from a limited partnership trade or business"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(e)(1), portfolio income—including interest, dividends, royalties, and gains from the disposition of portfolio assets—is expressly excluded from passive income. This prevents taxpayers from using passive losses to shelter investment returns. Choice A is incorrect because rental income is generally treated as passive income under Section 469(c)(2), not portfolio income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Identifying the $25,000 special allowance threshold
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'What is the maximum annual rental loss that an active participant may deduct against nonpassive income under the Section 469(i) special allowance?', '["$10,000", "$15,000", "$25,000", "$50,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(i)(2), taxpayers who actively participate in rental real estate activities may deduct up to $25,000 of rental losses against nonpassive income, subject to the AGI phase-out beginning at $100,000. Choice A is incorrect because the $25,000 threshold is the statutory maximum, not $10,000. The allowance phases out completely at $150,000 AGI.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Identifying what reduces at-risk amount
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under Section 465, which of the following reduces a taxpayer''s at-risk amount in an activity?', '["Additional cash contributions to the activity", "The taxpayer''s share of recourse debt", "Distributions received from the activity", "Qualified nonrecourse financing on real property"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 465(b), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount is reduced by distributions, withdrawals, and losses allowed in prior years. Cash contributions and recourse debt increase the at-risk amount. Choice A is incorrect because additional cash contributions increase rather than decrease the taxpayer''s amount at risk in the activity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether installment sale triggers suspended loss release
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Pemberton owns a passive activity with $120,000 of suspended losses and an adjusted basis of $85,000. In 2026, Pemberton sells the entire interest to an unrelated buyer for $200,000, structured as an installment sale with $50,000 received in year one and $150,000 over the next three years. Evaluate how the installment method affects the release of suspended passive losses.', '["All $120,000 of suspended losses is released in the year of sale because the disposition is fully taxable regardless of payment timing", "Only $30,000 of suspended losses is released in year one, proportionate to the $50,000 payment received", "The suspended losses are released ratably over the installment period as payments are collected", "No suspended losses are released until the final installment payment is received"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), a fully taxable disposition of an entire interest in a passive activity releases all suspended losses in the year of disposition, regardless of whether the installment method is used to report the gain. The loss release is triggered by the disposition event, not payment collection. Choice B is incorrect because Section 469(g) does not prorate suspended loss release based on installment payments—the entire $120,000 is released in the year of sale.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L2 — Calculating phase-out of rental allowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Thornton, a single taxpayer, actively participates in a rental townhouse that produces a $25,000 loss in 2026. Thornton''s AGI before the rental loss is $135,000. What amount of the rental loss can Thornton deduct under the Section 469(i) special allowance?', '["$0 deductible", "$7,500", "$17,500", "$25,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(i)(3), the $25,000 allowance is reduced by 50% of AGI exceeding $100,000. Thornton''s AGI exceeds $100,000 by $35,000, so the reduction is $17,500 ($35,000 × 50%). The remaining allowance is $25,000 − $17,500 = $7,500. The deductible amount is the lesser of the $25,000 loss or the $7,500 remaining allowance. Choice D is incorrect because the phase-out reduces the allowance; at $135,000 AGI, only $7,500 remains available.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Determining passive treatment for S corp shareholder
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Rivera owns 100% of Solaris Inc., an S corporation that operates a restaurant. Rivera does not work at the restaurant and participates for only 40 hours during 2026. The S corporation reports $85,000 of net income. How is the $85,000 classified on Rivera''s individual return?', '["Passive income subject to the Section 469 rules", "Nonpassive ordinary business income", "Portfolio income excluded from passive activity limitations", "Self-employment income subject to FICA taxes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(1), a trade or business activity in which the taxpayer does not materially participate is treated as a passive activity. Rivera''s 40 hours does not meet any of the seven material participation tests under Temp. Reg. Section 1.469-5T. Therefore, the $85,000 is passive income. Choice B is incorrect because nonpassive treatment requires material participation, which Rivera has not established with only 40 hours of involvement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Calculating suspended loss after netting multiple activities
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Gallagher has three passive activities in 2026: Activity J generates $18,000 income, Activity K generates a $42,000 loss, and Activity L generates a $9,000 loss. Gallagher has $12,000 in suspended passive losses from Activity K carried from 2025. What is Gallagher''s total suspended passive loss at year-end 2026?', '["$33,000", "$45,000", "$21,000", "$63,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(d), passive losses first offset passive income. The 2026 net passive loss is $42,000 + $9,000 − $18,000 = $33,000. Adding the $12,000 suspended loss carried from 2025 yields total suspended losses of $45,000 at year-end. Choice A is incorrect because it omits the $12,000 prior-year suspended losses that carry forward under Section 469(b) and must be included in the year-end total.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Identifying the 750-hour real estate professional test
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(7), how many hours must a taxpayer spend in real property trades or businesses to meet the first prong of the real estate professional test?', '["250 hours", "500 hours", "750 hours", "1,000 hours"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(7)(B), a taxpayer qualifies as a real estate professional if more than 750 hours of services are performed in real property trades or businesses and those hours constitute more than half of the taxpayer''s total personal services. Choice B is incorrect because the 500-hour threshold applies to the general material participation test under Temp. Reg. Section 1.469-5T, not the real estate professional qualification.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate related-party sale and suspended loss disallowance
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hargrove has $95,000 of suspended passive losses from a rental property with an adjusted basis of $180,000 and FMV of $230,000. Hargrove is considering selling the property to her adult son for $230,000. Evaluate whether this sale will release the suspended passive losses under the disposition rules.', '["The sale releases all $95,000 because a sale to a family member at FMV is a fully taxable disposition", "The sale does not release the suspended losses because a related-party sale under Section 267 is not a qualifying disposition under Section 469(g)", "The sale releases only the gain portion ($50,000) and the remaining $45,000 stays suspended", "The sale releases the losses but they are recharacterized as capital losses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1)(B), suspended passive losses are not released upon a disposition to a related party as defined under Section 267(b). A parent-child relationship is a related-party transaction under Section 267(b)(1). The suspended losses transfer to the son and are triggered when the son subsequently disposes of the property to an unrelated party. Choice A is incorrect because even though FMV is paid, the related-party rule overrides the general disposition release—Section 469(g) specifically excludes transfers to Section 267 related parties.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Simple at-risk amount calculation with cash and recourse debt
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Fitzgerald invests $35,000 cash in a partnership and personally guarantees $25,000 of partnership recourse debt. What is Fitzgerald''s at-risk amount under Section 465?', '["$25,000", "$35,000", "$60,000", "$10,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 465(b)(1) and (2), a taxpayer''s at-risk amount includes cash contributed plus amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer is personally liable (recourse debt). Fitzgerald''s at-risk amount is $35,000 cash plus $25,000 recourse debt, totaling $60,000. Choice B is incorrect because it excludes the $25,000 recourse debt that Fitzgerald personally guarantees, which increases the at-risk amount.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Evaluate strategy of electing out of installment method to maximize loss utilization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Mercer-Hale LLC is a passive activity held by Chen with $140,000 of suspended losses. Chen plans to sell the entire interest (basis $100,000) for $260,000 with $60,000 down and a $200,000 five-year note. Chen also has $80,000 of passive income from other activities in 2026. Evaluate whether Chen should elect out of the installment method under Section 453(d) to optimize loss utilization.', '["Electing out is unnecessary because all $140,000 of suspended losses are released in the year of sale regardless of installment treatment", "Electing out accelerates gain recognition to $160,000 in year one, fully absorbing the $140,000 suspended losses and leaving only $20,000 taxable gain", "Electing out is inadvisable because it triggers the full $160,000 gain immediately without releasing any additional suspended losses", "Electing out defers the suspended losses until the note is fully collected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(1), a fully taxable disposition of an entire passive activity interest releases all suspended losses in the year of sale, regardless of whether the installment method is used. Chen''s $140,000 of suspended losses is released in year one under either method. The installment election decision should focus on gain timing, not loss release. Choice B is incorrect because it implies the installment method would delay loss release—Section 469(g) releases all suspended losses upon disposition regardless of payment method.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L2 — Identifying passive vs nonpassive for material participant
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Quinn operates a landscaping business as a sole proprietor, working 1,800 hours in 2026. The business generates a $28,000 net loss. How is the loss classified under Section 469?', '["Passive loss limited to passive income offsets", "Nonpassive loss deductible against all income sources", "Portfolio loss deductible only against portfolio income", "Suspended loss carried forward to future years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(1), a trade or business activity is passive only if the taxpayer does not materially participate. Quinn''s 1,800 hours exceeds the 500-hour threshold under Temp. Reg. Section 1.469-5T(a)(1), establishing material participation. The $28,000 loss is therefore nonpassive. Choice A is incorrect because Quinn materially participates, so the passive activity rules do not limit the loss deduction.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Evaluate net investment income tax interaction with passive activity recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Sterling, a single taxpayer with AGI of $280,000, has $60,000 of passive income from Granite Partners LP and $25,000 of rental losses from an actively managed property. Sterling also has $45,000 of portfolio income. A tax advisor suggests Sterling regroup activities to recharacterize the partnership income as nonpassive. Evaluate the net investment income tax consequences of this regrouping.', '["Regrouping reduces net investment income by $60,000 because nonpassive business income is excluded from the Section 1411 NIIT base", "Regrouping has no effect on NIIT because both passive and nonpassive income are included in net investment income", "Regrouping increases NIIT liability because it eliminates the passive loss offset against the partnership income", "Regrouping reduces NIIT because the rental losses can then offset the reclassified nonpassive income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 1411(c), net investment income includes passive income but excludes nonpassive trade or business income in which the taxpayer materially participates. If Sterling regroups and materially participates, the $60,000 is excluded from the NIIT base, reducing the 3.8% tax by up to $2,280. Choice C is incorrect because removing the income from the NIIT base provides a larger tax benefit than the passive loss offset—the rental losses remain deductible under Section 469(i) regardless of the regrouping.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of death on suspended passive losses
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Caldwell dies on August 10, 2026, owning a rental property with $55,000 of suspended passive losses. The property has a date-of-death FMV of $320,000 and an adjusted basis of $280,000, resulting in a $40,000 step-up in basis. How are the suspended passive losses treated?', '["All $55,000 is deductible on Caldwell''s final return", "Only $15,000 is deductible on Caldwell''s final return", "The full $55,000 transfers to the estate and offsets estate income", "All $55,000 is permanently lost at death"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(g)(2), when a taxpayer dies, suspended passive losses are allowed on the final return only to the extent they exceed the step-up in basis. The $40,000 basis step-up absorbs $40,000 of the $55,000 suspended losses, leaving $15,000 deductible on Caldwell''s final return. Choice A is incorrect because Section 469(g)(2) specifically reduces the allowable loss by the amount of any basis increase at death.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Identifying per se passive rental activity
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(2), how is a rental activity generally classified regardless of the taxpayer''s level of participation?', '["As nonpassive if the taxpayer materially participates", "As a passive activity regardless of participation level", "As portfolio income excluded from passive rules", "As a trade or business subject to self-employment tax"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(c)(2), rental activities are treated as per se passive regardless of the taxpayer''s level of participation, with limited exceptions for real estate professionals under Section 469(c)(7). This means even a taxpayer who spends significant time managing a rental cannot avoid passive classification absent the real estate professional exception. Choice A is incorrect because material participation does not override the per se passive classification for rental activities.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — Analyzing effect of partnership loss on at-risk and passive ordering
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Monroe is a limited partner in Aspen Creek Partners with a tax basis of $200,000, an at-risk amount of $130,000, and does not materially participate. Monroe''s 2026 distributive share is a $175,000 loss. After applying all three loss limitation rules in order, what is the deductible amount and what amount is suspended at each level?', '["$130,000 deductible; $45,000 suspended at basis level, $0 at-risk, $0 passive", "$130,000 deductible; $0 at basis, $45,000 at-risk, $0 passive", "$0 deductible; $0 at basis, $0 at-risk, $175,000 passive", "$0 deductible; $0 at basis, $45,000 at-risk, $130,000 passive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The three limitations apply sequentially: basis (Section 704(d)), at-risk (Section 465), then passive (Section 469). The $175,000 loss passes the $200,000 basis limit. The at-risk limit of $130,000 suspends $45,000 under Section 465. The remaining $130,000 is then subject to passive rules—because Monroe does not materially participate and has no passive income, the entire $130,000 is suspended under Section 469. Choice B is incorrect because it treats the at-risk-cleared amount as deductible, ignoring the passive activity limitation that applies next.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Determining if 100-hour/significant participation test is met
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Patel participates in four significant participation activities during 2026: Activity M (180 hours), Activity N (210 hours), Activity O (90 hours), and Activity P (140 hours). In which activities is Patel treated as materially participating under the significant participation activity aggregation test?', '["All four activities because total hours exceed 500", "Only Activities M, N, and P because each exceeds 100 hours", "Activities M and N only because each individually exceeds 150 hours", "None of the activities because no single activity reaches 500 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Temp. Reg. Section 1.469-5T(a)(4), if a taxpayer participates in significant participation activities (each with more than 100 hours) and total participation across all such activities exceeds 500 hours, the taxpayer materially participates in each. Activities M (180), N (210), and P (140) each exceed 100 hours, totaling 530 hours. Activity O (90 hours) does not qualify. Choice A is incorrect because Activity O falls below the 100-hour threshold and cannot be included in the aggregation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Calculating loss allowed after AGI phase-out for married filing jointly
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Hoffman and Reyes, married filing jointly, actively participate in a rental property that generates a $25,000 loss in 2026. Their combined AGI before the rental loss is $142,000. What amount of the rental loss can they deduct under the special allowance?', '["$0 deductible", "$4,000", "$17,000", "$25,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 469(i)(3), the $25,000 special allowance is reduced by 50% of AGI exceeding $100,000. Hoffman and Reyes''s AGI exceeds $100,000 by $42,000, so the reduction is $42,000 × 50% = $21,000. The remaining allowance is $25,000 − $21,000 = $4,000. The deductible rental loss is the lesser of $25,000 or the $4,000 remaining allowance. Choice D is incorrect because at AGI of $142,000, the phase-out reduces the $25,000 allowance by $21,000, leaving only $4,000 available.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: hard / L3 — Analyzing effect of self-charged interest on passive income recharacterization
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Donovan lends $200,000 to Willow Creek LLC, a passive activity partnership in which she is a limited partner. In 2026, the partnership pays Donovan $16,000 of interest on the loan. The partnership also generates a $50,000 passive loss allocable to Donovan. Under the self-charged interest rules, how should the $16,000 interest be treated?', '["As portfolio income that cannot offset the passive loss", "As passive income under Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-7 to the extent of allocable passive deductions", "As nonpassive business income excluded from passive activity limitations", "As a Section 707(c) preferential payment reported separately from passive activity income"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.469-7, when a taxpayer lends money to a pass-through entity in which the taxpayer holds a passive interest, the interest income is recharacterized as passive income to the extent of the taxpayer''s allocable share of the partnership''s passive interest deduction. This prevents the mismatch where interest expense is passive but income is portfolio. Choice A is incorrect because the self-charged interest rules specifically override the default portfolio classification to prevent inequitable results.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Analyzing former passive activity treatment after change in participation
(6, 'Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules', 'Pearson had $48,000 of suspended passive losses from a consulting business in which she did not materially participate from 2023 through 2025. In 2026, Pearson increases her participation to 1,200 hours and the business generates $70,000 of income. How are the prior suspended losses and current income treated?', '["The $48,000 suspended losses offset the $70,000 income, leaving $22,000 of nonpassive income", "The $48,000 remains suspended because it arose in passive years and cannot offset current nonpassive income", "The $70,000 is recharacterized as passive income to absorb the $48,000 suspended losses", "The $48,000 is deductible only against other passive income sources, not the now-nonpassive consulting income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 469(f)(1), when a former passive activity becomes nonpassive due to material participation, suspended losses from prior passive years are allowed against the current income from that activity. Pearson''s $48,000 of suspended losses offsets the $70,000 of income, leaving $22,000 of net nonpassive income. Choice B is incorrect because Section 469(f)(1) specifically provides that prior suspended losses are released against income from the same activity when the taxpayer begins materially participating.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
